[00:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The dream of a robot foundation model makes possible that you can just walk up to a robot and get it to do any task almost immediately and generalize that behavior. Our new model Gen1-5 is an immediate learning'

[00:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generalist. It\'s a one-shot learner. It can learn and generalize new tasks in seconds prompted in context. It can compose prompts together to learn longer horizon tasks.'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It can be prompted from a simulator and transfer those behaviors into the real world. And in some cases, it can just watch humans and mimic them immediately on the spot.'

[00:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'For few-shot learning, it can learn new tasks with as few as one to 10 gradient steps on 1 to 5 minutes of data. And it\'s showing physical generalization including improvising how to use new'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tools to accomplish tasks. And it does all of these things out of the box. The fastest way it learns is with zero training on a new task and just a few'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'seconds of demonstration data put into the model\'s context. We show the robot what to do and it generalizes it. We call this physical prompting. We\'ve done variations of in-context'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompting for about a year now, but what we\'re seeing for the first time is a very generalized in-context learning capability. These tasks are simple today and while the success rates are not super high,'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we\'ve never seen a model that can do this before. It\'s not something we explicitly trained for, but has emerged from our new training recipe. Beyond its context, it can also learn'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with extremely few training steps. And among the new levels of physical generalization we are seeing is novel tool use improvisation. In one example, we taught it to use a'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'brush to sweep a block into a bowl. And when we gave it a banana instead, it could figure out how to turn the banana into an impromptu brush.'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And when we gave it a dustpan, it used its other hand to nudge the block onto the dustpan, and then raised the block up and dumped it into the bowl.'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It could also sweep multiple objects and and figure out how to switch hands ambidextrously. It\'s overall showing more improvisational intelligence than previous models.'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It had a LEGO brick stuck on one of its hands, and it used its other hand to remove it. We had taught it to put blocks into a bowl, but then we covered the bowl with a piece of paper, and it removed the piece of paper to accomplish its task.'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'We taught it to open a jar lid with one hand, and then it just did it with two hands. And when we gave it different bottles and cups, it figured out how to open those, too.'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; What do you do with the knife, though? &gt;&gt; We\'re also starting to see human-to-robot in-context learning emerge, where a person can just show the robot what to do with their own human hands, and the robot mimics it on the'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'spot with its hands. We are still in the very early days, but we\'re tremendously excited about the potential of this new frontier of intelligence in robot foundation models.'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; No.'
